<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ivan Meštrović</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a>–<a href="/years/1962">1962</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ivan Meštrović was a Croatian sculptor and architect, one of the leading sculptors of the twentieth century. A supporter of South Slav unity and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-committee">Yugoslav Committee</a>, he created monumental works including the Monument to the Unknown Hero on Mount Avala, the Victor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> and the statue of Gregory of Nin in Split. He emigrated to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ivan-mestrovic.jpg" alt="Ivan Meštrović" width="320" height="431" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Grantham Bain Collection (Library of Congress) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ivan_Me%C5%A1trovic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ivan Meštrović was born on 15 August 1883 in Vrpolje, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavonia">Slavonia</a>, and grew up in the Dalmatian village of Otavice, where his father was a poor peasant and sheep-breeder. At sixteen he became an apprentice to the stonemason Pavle Bilinić in Split. A mine owner from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> paid for him to study at the Academy of Fine Arts Vienna, where his teachers were Edmund von Hellmer and Otto Wagner. In 1905 he first exhibited with the Secession Group. In 1908 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, and in 1911 his work for the Serbian Pavilion at the Rome International Exhibition won the grand prix.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After a journey to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, Meštrović became a supporter of Yugoslav unity and was called the Prophet of Yugoslavism. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a> cycle of 1908 to 1910 revived national myth, and he was a founding member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslav-committee">Yugoslav Committee</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. After the war he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a> in 1922 and became professor and later director of its Academy of Fine Arts. His monuments of the 1920s and 1930s include Gregory of Nin in Split, the Victor on the Kalemegdan fortress and the Monument to the Unknown Hero on Avala, and he designed the Račić family mausoleum at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cavtat">Cavtat</a>.</p><p>On 7 November 1941 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ustase">Ustaše</a> arrested him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagreb">Zagreb</a>, and he spent three and a half months in prison. With the help of Archbishop Aloysius Stepinac and the Vatican he was released and left for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> in 1943. After the war he refused <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a>'s government's invitation to return, and in 1946 Syracuse University gave him a professorship; he taught at the University of Notre Dame from 1955.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1952 he signed his Croatian estates over to the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, with more than 400 sculptures. He died on 16 January 1962 in South Bend, Indiana, and was buried in the family mausoleum at Otavice. The Ivan Meštrović Gallery in Split holds 86 statues given in 1950. Alonzo Lansford wrote after his 1947 exhibition at the Metropolitan Museum of Art that American sculptors of all schools revered him. He also wrote essays, short stories and memoirs.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ivan Meštrović” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In culture, Serbian artists including <b>Ivan Meštrović</b> showed in the Serbian pavilion at the International Exhibition of Art of 1911.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Belgrade, Zagreb and Ljubljana nonetheless grew as modern cities, and writers such as Ivo Andrić and Miroslav Krleža and the sculptor <b>Ivan Meštrović</b> were active in the period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Vrpolje, in what is now <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>. Died in South Bend, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.21,18.41&amp;year=1934" aria-label="Vrpolje on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Ivan Meštrović"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4306 6190h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4306 6190h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.21,18.41&amp;year=1934">What was here in 1934? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
